1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
 After tax economic analysis

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
 subject of this chapter

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:13,000
 What we want to see is what is the impact of tax on our business

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,000
 So far we've done before tax analysis

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
 now we want to do an after tax analysis

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,000
 As you will see the only difference between the two is the tax

7
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,000
 it's not that we for a replacement, an after tax replacement for instance

8
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:35,000
 will have a totally new approach or method that is not anything like the one we had when we did not consider tax

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:38,000
 So only difference will be the tax

10
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:42,000
 Let's have a look at the terminology

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:49,000
 By the way, of course, taxes are real cash flows

12
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
 You will see that we use depreciation in computing our taxes

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 but these depreciations are only used for computing our taxes, they are not cash flows

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
 So what is important is that we know our net operating income

15
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,000
 which are gross revenues minus our operating expenses

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 Gross revenue, gross income represented by GIOE for our operating expenses

17
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:22,000
 we have been encountering that one before

18
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
 Taxable income is then the gross revenue minus our operating expenses

19
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 This one, sorry, this is this

20
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
 but also we can deduct our depreciation

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 so that means that the taxable income becomes lower

22
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
 What else do I need to say?

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:49,000
 I think that then what are the taxes?

24
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:54,000
 The taxes are then your tax rate times your taxable income

25
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:01,000
 and your net profit X after taxes obviously is then our taxable income minus our taxes

26
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 Tax rates, I already hinted to it when we talked about depreciation and taxes in the previous chapter

27
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
 In some countries we have different tax levels

28
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:21,000
 We have a local tax level for instance for a state or a province

29
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:27,000
 and there are federal rates and we have to make one tax rate out of that

30
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
 and that's what we are going to do

31
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
 We are only going to look at effective tax rates here

32
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,000
 What you see to the right, the table is not recent

33
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:43,000
 That's what was mentioned in the book

34
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:49,000
 But what it's illustrating is that tax rates can be quite different from country to country

35
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,000
 and although this is out of scope of this course

36
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:06,000
 it's good to mention that taxes can be an important argument for companies to start a production plant

37
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,000
 in a given country for instance or to locate their headquarters in another country

38
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 So it's an important aspect but we will not pursue that in this course

39
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
 Let's have our cash flow before taxes

40
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:29,000
 Cash flow before taxes is gross income or revenues minus operating expenses

41
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
 minus any investment plus any salvage value that there might be

42
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:38,000
 And after taxes we have this amount

43
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 We have as we saw before

44
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:45,000
 We have of course this before

45
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:49,000
 But then we have our taxes that we have to deduct

46
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:57,000
 and the taxes that we have to deduct or determined by our gross income, operating expenses

47
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,000
 and also by our depreciation

48
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:05,000
 Now what about the effect of taxes

49
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:11,000
 What about the effect on taxes by the depreciation method and the recovery method

50
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:19,000
 Well as you see here all methods, all depreciation methods will lead to the same amount of total taxes

51
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
 and here also all lengths will have the same amount of total taxes

52
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
 But what is important of course is the present worth of the taxes

53
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
 Now let's have an example

54
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:38,000
 So this is an example about a Mexican manufacturing company

55
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,000
 It's a multinational and they are also active in Germany

56
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:48,000
 The company just purchased an asset for 90,000 with an estimated useful life of 9 years

57
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:55,000
 However the German tax law allows a shorter recovery period, namely of 5 years

58
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:58,000
 Their net income is 30,000 per year

59
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 and now we are asked to demonstrate the tax advantage for the shorter period

60
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:06,000
 5 years instead of 9 years

61
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:10,000
 with an effective tax rate of 35%

62
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:15,000
 and if invested money is returning a 5% per year after taxes

63
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:23,000
 We should not take into account any salvage value and a classic straight line depreciation is to be used

64
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
 Let's have a look

65
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
 So for 9 years straight line depreciation

66
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:36,000
 we know that the amount of depreciation every year will be 90,000 divided by the number of years is 10,000

67
00:05:36,000 --> 00:05:43,000
 Taxable income is our income per year

68
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:46,000
 Minus our operating expenses is 20,000

69
00:05:46,000 --> 00:06:00,000
 Taxable income is our income minus our depreciation

70
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
 Tax rate times that taxable income is 7,000

71
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,000
 and it's for every year that we have to consider that

72
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:20,000
 So that means that the present worth of taxes will be 49,755

73
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,000
 and the total of taxes that we have to pay, well every year we pay 7,000 so that's easy

74
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:29,000
 That's 63,000

75
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:35,000
 Now if we go to the other system with the shorter recovery period

76
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:45,000
 We of course have the same formulas but now we have our depreciation for the first 5 years is 18,000

77
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:49,000
 because we are allowed to have that accelerated depreciation

78
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
 and we will have no depreciation for years 6 to 9

79
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:03,000
 So our taxes will be the tax rate times our income minus our depreciation

80
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,000
 4,200 for the first 5 years

81
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:15,000
 and well we will have to pay on the full 30,000 for years 6 to 9

82
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:20,000
 So the present worth of our taxes will be the 4,200

83
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:24,000
 We pay the first 5 years, we have the 10,500

84
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:28,000
 which we pay for the next years

85
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:32,000
 and we make it a present worth as well

86
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:37,000
 So here we have a present worth of 47,000

87
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:44,000
 There we have a present worth of 49,755

88
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:49,000
 The total taxes here was 63

89
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:58,000
 Here the total taxes are 5 times the 10,500

90
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:04,000
 and is again the 36,000 as was mentioned in the previous part

